Z||000004||FieroJoe||01-18-2001||08:49 AM||||87 Coupe, one in my sig file.  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||mbramble||01-18-2001||10:45 AM||||I have an '87 Coupe that did not have electric trunk release but has 'ajar' switch.  I got a solenoid, and connecting wire from an older car that didn't have 'ajar' switch.  It had a single wire that went to the solenoid.<P>The car has a 4 conductor connector on the car side with 4 wires running thru it.  On the trunk side, only three of the wires are there.  By doing some checking with an voltmeter, I found that the 4th wire on the car side of the connector came from the relay for the trunk release.  So, I put a pin similar to what is used in the trunk side connector onto the wire coming from the solenoid (which I fished thru the deck lid) and installed it in the connector from the decklid.  I mounted the ajar assmebly onto the solenoid and then mounted it to the trunk lid.<P>Car-----Decklid<BR>x-----&gt; &gt;-----x <BR>x-----&gt; &gt;-----x  <BR>x-----&gt; &gt;-----x  <BR>x-----&gt; &gt;------------------S<P>x = existing wires in my '87 Coupe<P>S = the wire I installed from solenoid to connector using a pin similar to the ones in the connector.  I can now simply unplg the decklid wiring from the car wiring.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by mbramble (edited 01-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||FieroJoe||01-18-2001||03:25 PM||||Thanks, mbramble.  That was exactly what I was wanting to do.  Where did you get a pin that could fit in there?  Is it a standard type, something that I could get at autozone or something?||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||01-18-2001||08:33 PM||||take the existing wire out as described in my article. It will make adding the new wire easier if you snake it into the OE bundle then stuff it back in the deck lid.<P><BR>The connector is called a Weather-Pack connector. Yes a terminal can be added to an existing shell.<P>To start:<BR>You have 2 choices.<P>You can buy the needed terminal parts. They are in the MotorMite master catalog with all the other electrical stuff. You need the correct terminal and the silicone plug. They are sold seperatly. (Then you have to fool with trying to get e reliable crimp or solder it on.)<P>OR<P>You can get a spare Weather-Pack connector with some wire on it and Carefully break the shell apart with wire cutters to free a terminal. (make sure you are taking out a terminal that matches the other 3...) The more wire you get with the connector the better. Note: it doesn't matter how many terminals are in the spare shell. just that they are the tubular ones and not the pins. Look in the connector, you'll see what I mean.<P>The second is the better choice for most people. You can just extend it to reach the deck latch. The more wire on the spare the better, then you can hide the splice up in the deck lid. (Any junk GM car will have these connectors.)<P>Next:<P>Carefully open the door on the back of the shell. There is a latch on either side you have to pry up.<P>There may be a silicone plug in the spare hole on the connector in the car now. Remove it.<P>Insert the new terminal into the shell.<P>put an apropriate crimp terminal on the other end of the lead. then stuff the whole mess back into the deck lid. plug it all in and you are done.<P>If you use a terminal chopped out of a spare shell the whole thing should only take 20-30 minutes max to do.<P>MARK THE LATCH LOCATION before you remove it from the deck lid!<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 01-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||FieroJoe||01-19-2001||12:04 PM||||Last night I got all the wiring done, and adjustment done, and all I have to do now is get the weather-pack to seal correctly.<BR>  I found a 20-22 gauge butt connector without insulation that would fit great in the extra spot, and make a connection on the other side, but now the connector dosent seem to want to go back together all the way.  Is the green ribbed insulation around the outside of the plastic shell supposed to be entirely concealed?  I can't get it to go all the way together, with or without the new connector in there.<BR>  Basically, the problem is that I cant get it to ground.  I can get my new terminal to make a connection, I grounded the frame of the solenoid and pushed the button and it fired, but it dosent want to ground through the connector.  I may have to go out and get an actual thing from a GM connector.<P>Anyways, thanks to both of you for all your help, I hope I can get it to work soon!||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||01-19-2001||07:23 PM||||That's because it doesn't ground thru the connector. it will only fire with the lid shut. it grounds to the latch ring.||||reg||1
Z||000010||FieroJoe||01-19-2001||08:23 PM||||Yeah, I found that out today, after making sure my ajar light was coming on for it and the trunk courtesy light.  I couldnt find a new weather pack connector at the junkyard, mostly becuase all the cars are gutted and torn apart before they are put out, and all there was, for wiring that is, was just the male or female ends.  I couldnt find any to match.  Then, while walking back, I noticed a connector that was still attached on some car (I think it was a truck, but it had no chassis or body, just an engine compartment).  I looked, and it had 4 wires, so I took it with about 6" of wire on each side.  Wired it up, wrapped all the wires in new tubing, and zip-tied it to the hinge frame.  Looks good to me, and I think the connector that I got is Ford's version of a weather pack.||||reg||1
Z||000011||theogre||01-20-2001||12:01 AM||||It didn't have to be an identical connector. They come in 1-6 pin variaties. any would work as long as it was the right half.<P>If you got it closed enough to latch then it's all the way in. The green seal often shifts a bit so that the last ridge isn't inside the shell. that's normal.<BR>||||reg||1
